+## Batching: Parallel Where Independent, Sequential Where Dependent
+
+Read the plan's `## Task Dependency Graph` section to compute batches:
+
+- A **batch** is a set of tasks whose `depends on` lists are all satisfied (every predecessor's review passed).
+- The first batch is every task with `depends on: none`. Dispatch ALL of them in parallel — one Task tool call per task in the same response. Use the agent types from `## Agent Assignments`.
+- After every task in the current batch passes its per-task two-stage review (spec compliance + code quality), compute the next batch (every pending task whose predecessors are now complete) and dispatch that batch in parallel.
+- Continue until all tasks are done. A plan with no parallelism is just a chain of single-task batches.
+
+**Why this matters:** parallel dispatch of independent tasks is the whole point of subagents. Don't run independent tasks one-at-a-time — that wastes the orchestration model. But never speculatively parallelise tasks that share files or consume each other's output; that causes merge conflicts and broken interfaces. Default to sequential when independence is not obvious from the dependency graph.
+
+**Single-task plans:** trivially one batch with one dispatch. No parallelism overhead, but still via subagent — never inline.
+
+**If the plan lacks `## Task Dependency Graph`:** default every task to "depends on prior task" (full sequential chain) and note the gap so writing-plans can add the section next time. Better safe-sequential than unsafe-parallel.
+
+## Subagent Type Selection
+
+The `subagent_type` for each implementer comes from the plan's `## Agent Assignments` section (copied from the spec). Do NOT re-decide during execution. If the plan lacks an assignment for a task, fall back to the table below based on the task's primary language/domain:
+
+| Domain / Language      | subagent_type                        |
+|------------------------|--------------------------------------|
+| Rust                   | rust-engineer                        |
+| Go                     | systems-programming:golang-pro       |
+| Python                 | python-development:python-pro        |
+| Bevy game / engine     | bevy-engineer                        |
+| Unity game / C#        | unity-csharp-engineer                |
+| Solidity / Web3 / DeFi | blockchain-web3:blockchain-developer |
+| Security audit         | security-scanning:security-auditor   |
+| Code review            | git-pr-workflows:code-reviewer       |
+| General / other        | general-purpose (default)            |
+
+For code quality reviews of Web3/Solidity code, use `git-pr-workflows:code-reviewer` and additionally dispatch `security-scanning:security-auditor` for a parallel security review.
+
+For Bevy projects (any crate that depends on `bevy = ...` in `Cargo.toml` or imports `bevy::prelude::*`), pick `bevy-engineer` over `rust-engineer` — it carries Bevy ECS, scheduling, plugin, and migration conventions on top of the Rust baseline.
+
+For Unity projects (a `ProjectSettings/ProjectVersion.txt`, an `Assets/` + `Packages/manifest.json` layout, `.asmdef` files, or `.cs` files importing `UnityEngine`), pick `unity-csharp-engineer` for any C# gameplay task — it carries MonoBehaviour lifecycle, ScriptableObject, GC/Jobs performance, and unity-mcp Editor-loop conventions on top of the C# baseline.
+
+## Plan Checkbox State (resume-safe progress log)
+
+The plan file's `- [ ]` / `- [x]` checkboxes are the durable, cross-session record of what's done, complementing the ledger. The controller (you) is responsible for keeping them accurate.
+
+**At plan load (before dispatching anything):**
+1. `grep -nE '^- \[(x| )\]' <plan-file>` to enumerate every step and its state.
+2. For every step already `- [x]`: mark its TodoWrite todo as completed and never include that step in any implementer prompt. The implementer must not see done steps in its task description.
+3. If every step in a task is `- [x]`, that task is done — skip it entirely. Move to the next task with pending steps.
+4. If checkbox state is internally inconsistent (later steps `- [x]` while earlier steps in the same task are `- [ ]`, or `- [x]` next to obviously missing artifacts on disk), pause and surface this to the human partner before starting. Do NOT silently re-run or "trust the checkboxes".
+5. Pending steps (`- [ ]`) seed the TodoWrite list and the implementer prompts.
+
+**After a task passes spec review + code quality review + prose cleanup (and BEFORE marking the TodoWrite todo complete):**
+1. Edit the plan file: flip every `- [ ]` belonging to that task's steps to `- [x]`. Use Edit on the literal `- [ ]` prefix per step line. Do not reformat surrounding lines.
+2. Commit the plan file edit (alongside any final cleanup commits, or as its own commit). The commit message can be terse: `plan: mark Task N complete`.
+3. Then mark the TodoWrite todo complete and proceed to the next task.
+
+**If the implementer reported flipping individual step boxes during execution (it should — see implementer-prompt.md):**
+- Verify the boxes match what was actually implemented before you start review. A `- [x]` next to unverified work is a bug; flip it back and re-run.
+- Do NOT double-flip steps the implementer already flipped. Only flip ones still `- [ ]`.
+
+**Never:**
+- Author or accept new pre-checked `- [x]` boxes (those are skip-this signals; manufacturing them skips real work).
+- Batch all flips at the end of the whole plan — the resume point is lost.
+- Touch unrelated checkboxes in the plan file.
+
