+## Prose Cleanup Pass
+
+After code quality review passes (0 critical/medium) and before flipping the plan's checkboxes for the task, run a prose cleanup pass on the task's changed files. This is a quick scan, not a full review.
+
+Trim comments hard first, then humanize what survives — in that order.
+
+**What to fix:**
+- Cut every low-value comment, not just AI-slop. Anything that restates the code ("// increment counter", "// return result"), narrates the obvious ("// First, set up the client"), or decorates (banners, dividers) goes — whoever wrote it. You have license to delete; when in doubt, delete. A wrong or stale comment is worse than none.
+- Strip comments that smell like AI generated them ("// Ensures proper handling of...", "// Leverages the X pattern to facilitate...")
+- Whatever *why* comments survive: rewrite them to sound human — direct, specific, no filler
+- Humanize docstrings: no "This function serves as...", no "facilitates", no "ensures". Just say what it does.
+- Humanize error messages: no "Failed to successfully...", no "Unable to properly..."
+- Humanize commit messages if they haven't been committed yet
+
+**What NOT to touch:**
+- Code logic, structure, tests
+- Comments that explain *why* (keep those, they're valuable)
+- License headers, generated code markers
+
+**How to dispatch:** Send the implementer subagent a focused cleanup prompt with the list of changed files. Keep it short — this is a 2-minute pass, not a rewrite.
