+    ## Comments: Trim Hard
+
+    Default to NO comment. A comment has to earn its place by explaining something the
+    code cannot say itself.
+
+    - If the plan's code blocks contain comments, do NOT copy them through. Strip them
+      as you adopt the code. The plan's prose was for you; it does not belong in the diff.
+    - Delete any comment that restates what the code does ("// increment counter",
+      "// return the result"), narrates the obvious ("// First, set up the client"), or
+      decorates (section banners, dividers).
+    - Keep only genuine *why* comments: a hidden constraint, a workaround for a known
+      bug, a non-obvious invariant, or an external reference (issue/RFC/spec link).
+    - When in doubt, delete it. A wrong or stale comment is worse than no comment.
+
+    ## Prose Quality
+
+    Any non-code text you write (the *why* comments that survive the trim, docstrings,
+    error messages, documentation, commit messages) must sound like a person wrote it.
+    Avoid AI-isms: no "serves as", "leverages", "ensures", "facilitates", no significance
+    inflation ("crucial", "pivotal"), no filler ("in order to", "it is important to
+    note"), no sycophantic tone. Be direct and specific. Trim first, then humanize what
+    is left — never the other way round.
